          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-53e793bcf6344e35f8a7",
          "document_id": "med-doc-789ce78a7120946a49ec",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "once a year. Have this exam even if your vision is okay. Learn more about eye disease and diabetes. Protect Your Kidneys High blood glucose and high blood pressure may damage the kidneys. Damaged kidneys do not do a good job of filtering out wastes and extra fluid. Here are ways to prevent diabetes kidney problems. - Keep your blood glucose and blood pressure as close to your target goal as you can. - Get tested at least once a year for kidney disease. Ask your doctor if you should be tested. - Follow the healthy eating plan you work out with your doctor or dietitian. If you already have kidney problems, your dietitian may suggest you cut back on",
          "score": 0.014932038389126141,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-789ce78a7120946a49ec/med-chunk-53e793bcf6344e35f8a7",
          "text_sha256": "c6d580a85b82245b8502049b7c5bd6eafca076384560bf6e6d90638d8296f74d",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": [
            "kg-node-fcb21efed59ffe4ef1ceac5c",
            "med-chunk-53e793bcf6344e35f8a7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-d823ba21282c7148a6c9",
          "document_id": "med-doc-e394863eadce10631150",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "Diabetes is a serious, life-long disease. It can lead to problems such as heart disease, stroke, vision loss, kidney disease, and nerve damage. More than 8 million people in the United States have type 2 diabetes and dont know it. Many people dont find out they have diabetes until they are faced with problems such as blurry vision or heart trouble. Certain factors can increase your risk for diabetes, and its important to know what they are. Type 1 Diabetes Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease. In an autoimmune reaction, antibodies, or immune cells, attach to the bodys own healthy tissues by mistake, signaling the body to attack them. At present, scientists do not know exactly what causes the",
          "score": 0.013988134964775677,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-e394863eadce10631150/med-chunk-d823ba21282c7148a6c9",
          "text_sha256": "a5fb081a21c052ee4b9c63667b5f9a42d2ca373e98fc1677f4bd72b46a4952e9",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": [
            "kg-node-fcb21efed59ffe4ef1ceac5c",
            "med-chunk-d823ba21282c7148a6c9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-a43cb96d1bc187e62f78",
          "document_id": "med-doc-b842fa97d1ec8758d0ec",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "puts you at a higher risk of getting type 2 diabetes. Over time, having too much glucose in your blood can cause serious problems. It can damage your eyes, kidneys, and nerves. Diabetes can also cause heart disease, stroke and even the need to remove a limb. Pregnant women can also get diabetes, called gestational diabetes. Blood tests can show if you have diabetes. One type of test, the A1C, can also check on how you are managing your diabetes. Exercise, weight control and sticking to your meal plan can help control your diabetes. You should also monitor your blood glucose level and take medicine if prescribed. NIH: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases",
          "score": 0.013843101343101342,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-b842fa97d1ec8758d0ec/med-chunk-a43cb96d1bc187e62f78",
          "text_sha256": "b548324612f9d86d65f9964556b4a53c5780a3373f06e6edb78f809caad891fc",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": [
            "kg-node-fcb21efed59ffe4ef1ceac5c",
            "med-chunk-a43cb96d1bc187e62f78"
          ]
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-f7c195b56a3d5b981b2b",
          "document_id": "med-doc-789ce78a7120946a49ec",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "out with your doctor or dietitian. If you already have kidney problems, your dietitian may suggest you cut back on protein. Keep your blood glucose and blood pressure as close to your target goal as you can. Get tested at least once a year for kidney disease. Ask your doctor if you should be tested. Follow the healthy eating plan you work out with your doctor or dietitian. If you already have kidney problems, your dietitian may suggest you cut back on protein. Learn more about keeping your kidneys healthy. Protect Your Skin Skin care is very important, too. Because people with diabetes may have more injuries and infections, they should protect their skin by keeping it clean and taking",
          "score": 0.01157995395127748,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-789ce78a7120946a49ec/med-chunk-f7c195b56a3d5b981b2b",
          "text_sha256": "9a019a7a06f6f55b15aa357f7275a18ffebff57ee9dd1de139d6b8c7f415ff48",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": [
            "kg-node-fcb21efed59ffe4ef1ceac5c",
            "med-chunk-f7c195b56a3d5b981b2b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-0655c67739d55eddba3b",
          "document_id": "med-doc-a5db0a28a75a649d2b0e",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "diabetes is the most common cause of kidney failure in the United States. People with kidney failure need either dialysis or a kidney transplant. You can slow down kidney damage or keep it from getting worse. Controlling your blood sugar and blood pressure, taking your medicines and not eating too much protein can help. NIH: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases",
          "score": 0.011475409836065573,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-a5db0a28a75a649d2b0e/med-chunk-0655c67739d55eddba3b",
          "text_sha256": "e468c2983be7f20564965a829dcfe6dade3344e05196bb21f76caf8c90d129fd",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": []
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-9da097cc8e0454044f86",
          "document_id": "med-doc-a5db0a28a75a649d2b0e",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "If you have diabetes, your blood glucose, or blood sugar, levels are too high. Over time, this can damage your kidneys. Your kidneys clean your blood. If they are damaged, waste and fluids build up in your blood instead of leaving your body. Kidney damage from diabetes is called diabetic nephropathy. It begins long before you have symptoms. An early sign of it is small amounts of protein in your urine. A urine test can detect it. A blood test can also help determine how well your kidneys are working. If the damage continues, your kidneys could fail. In fact, diabetes is the most common cause of kidney failure in the United States. People with kidney failure need either dialysis",
          "score": 0.011290322580645162,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-a5db0a28a75a649d2b0e/med-chunk-9da097cc8e0454044f86",
          "text_sha256": "02bfba6178d688aef62041f1104f953b54207acd439214bce08dfab44177946e",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": []
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-e100f96a16ab88902cf3",
          "document_id": "med-doc-b34827865b9453aa3abe",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "urine contains more than 30 milligrams of albumin per gram of creatinine, with or without decreased eGFR. The ADA and the NIH recommend annual assessment of urine albumin excretion to assess kidney damage in all people with type 2 diabetes and people who have had type 1 diabetes for 5 years or more. If kidney disease is detected, it should be addressed as part of a comprehensive approach to the treatment of diabetes.",
          "score": 0.010932260228034875,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-b34827865b9453aa3abe/med-chunk-e100f96a16ab88902cf3",
          "text_sha256": "1d7a9199d771e005586dc466b16ee330bf38d594ec242d080bbd446eb4bb928a",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": []
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-51fc2d8e96ea4372aca3",
          "document_id": "med-doc-c010a12508a2f3c90c12",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "care provider or at least two to four times a year - learn about possible benefits from taking an ACE inhibitor or an ARB if a person has high blood pressure - measure eGFR at least once a year to check kidney function - get the amount of protein in the urine tested at least once a year to check for kidney damage",
          "score": 0.01074486301369863,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-c010a12508a2f3c90c12/med-chunk-51fc2d8e96ea4372aca3",
          "text_sha256": "e1401543e02d5f0980bea2d553a11ec7f2cbc1e002501d91f3f32f98d52117fc",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": []
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-9450fd91fe8f2ef08104",
          "document_id": "med-doc-69d7e1df38be1ee02882",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "is a mineral that helps your heartbeat stay regular and muscles work right. Healthy kidneys keep the right amount of potassium in your body. However, if you have severe kidney damage, high levels of potassium may cause an abnormal heart rhythm or even make your heart stop, called cardiac arrest. Some high-potassium foods include apricots, bananas, oranges, and potatoes. More information about healthy eating and kidney disease is provided in the NIDDK health topics: - Eat Right to Feel Right on Hemodialysis - Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults - Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults - What I need to know about Eating and Diabetes",
          "score": 0.010701357466063348,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-69d7e1df38be1ee02882/med-chunk-9450fd91fe8f2ef08104",
          "text_sha256": "e77fbe73381f72f57c62e419326c734fa5813dcefbff480f3ee5ecb230850ee4",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": []
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-b6c5bcf0942e802bfd8b",
          "document_id": "med-doc-b12f825bec0780a091fd",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "vomiting, facial flushing, stomach pain, and dryness of the mouth (xerostomia). In severe cases, diabetic ketoacidosis can lead to coma and death. Over many years, the chronic high blood sugar associated with diabetes may cause damage to blood vessels and nerves, leading to complications affecting many organs and tissues. The retina, which is the light-sensitive tissue at the back of the eye, can be damaged (diabetic retinopathy), leading to vision loss and eventual blindness. Kidney damage (diabetic nephropathy) may also occur and can lead to kidney failure and end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Pain, tingling, and loss of normal sensation (diabetic neuropathy) often occur, especially in the feet. Impaired circulation and absence of the normal sensations that prompt reaction to injury",
          "score": 0.010406698564593302,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
